INT. GREENFIELD VETERINARY CLINIC - AFTER HOURS

Rain.

The waiting room. Empty chairs. A fish tank bubbling. A wall clock reads 11:48.

LUCAS, 20s, a vet tech in scrubs, kneels beside the reception desk, a flashlight in his teeth, looking at the floor. DR. ALMA PRUITT, 50s, stands over him holding a clipboard. MRS. ABERNATHY, 70s, in a raincoat, sits in a plastic chair with an empty cat carrier on her lap.

Silence.

DR. PRUITT
Lucas. Tell me again.

LUCAS
(taking the flashlight out)
She was in the carrier at nine. I checked her. I swear I checked her.

MRS. ABERNATHY
Biscuit doesn't just disappear.

Nothing.

Lucas stands. He crosses to the supply cabinet and opens it. Bandages. Syringes. A bag of kibble torn open, the floor under it dusted with crumbs. He crouches. Small paw prints in the powder of spilled flea treatment, leading toward the back hall.

LUCAS
Look.

Dr. Pruitt looks. Mrs. Abernathy rises, the carrier trembling in her hands. The prints stop at a closed door marked STAFF ONLY.

DR. PRUITT
That door's always locked.

LUCAS
Not tonight.

The knob. It turns by itself. A soft CLICK.

Mrs. Abernathy gasps. Dr. Pruitt steps in front of her. Lucas lifts the flashlight like a weapon.

The door swings open. Dark. Cold. The smell of antiseptic.

A small shape. Two green eyes.

And then, behind them, the front bell JANGLES. The glass door BANGS against the wall. Wind and rain flood in.

DEPUTY HARLOW, 40s, hat dripping, steps inside and shuts the door behind him. He looks at the three of them. At the open staff door. At the cat carrier.

DEPUTY HARLOW
Evening. Somebody called in a break-in at this address.

DR. PRUITT
(too fast)
No. No break-in.

LUCAS
Nobody called.

Silence.

DEPUTY HARLOW
Funny. The call came from inside.

The three of them turn, slowly, to the staff door. The small shape in the dark steps forward into the light. A gray cat. Biscuit. A tiny brass key hangs from her collar.

Nothing else moves.
